## Pure reducers, and Immer's drafts

A **reducer is pure**: given the same state and the same action, it produces the same result, with no side effects and no mutation of the original. Purity is what makes reducers trivially testable and what makes time-travel debugging possible at all.

Immer lets you *write* mutating syntax while *producing* immutable results:

```ts
const pickListSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'pickList',
  initialState,
  reducers: {
    itemPicked(state, action: PayloadAction<{ sku: string; qty: number }>) {
      const line = state.lines[action.payload.sku];
      if (line) line.pickedQty += action.payload.qty;   // looks like mutation
    },                                                   // is actually a draft
  },
});
```

The object you receive as `state` inside a reducer is a **draft** — a Proxy. Immer records what you do to it and produces a genuinely new, immutable state when the reducer returns. **Two things to know about drafts:** returning a value *and* mutating the draft in the same reducer is an error, and holding a reference to the draft **outside** the reducer causes a crash the moment it is read, because Immer revokes the Proxy once the reducer finishes.

## The selector that never hits its cache

```ts
const selectOpenPicksForZone = createSelector(
  [selectAllPicks, (_, zone: string) => zone],
  (picks, zone) => picks.filter(p => p.zone === zone && p.status === 'open')
);
```

This looks correctly memoized. It is — for **one** set of arguments. `createSelector`'s default cache holds exactly **one result**. If two components call this same selector instance with different `zone` values, every call alternates the cached arguments, so every call **misses**, recomputes, and returns a **new array reference** — which then defeats any downstream memoization relying on that reference staying stable too.

This is the same mechanism Section 1 taught with `Object.is`, wearing Redux's clothing: the array's *contents* might be identical between two calls, but its *reference* is new every time, because the selector recomputed from scratch. Fix it with a **per-component-instance selector factory**, so each component gets its own selector with its own single-result cache:

```ts
function makeSelectOpenPicksForZone() {
  return createSelector(
    [selectAllPicks, (_: RootState, zone: string) => zone],
    (picks, zone) => picks.filter(p => p.zone === zone && p.status === 'open')
  );
}

function ZonePickList({ zone }: { zone: string }) {
  const selectOpenPicks = useMemo(makeSelectOpenPicksForZone, []);
  const picks = useSelector((state: RootState) => selectOpenPicks(state, zone));
  // ...
}
```

